    "product_id": "0573b76b-82a7-4775-b6e0-f8f21bbf4ed3",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 241148\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n448 AM PDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north winds today with lingering shower and storm chances in \n  northwestern Arizona.\n\n* Quiet and seasonable conditions through at least Wednesday, with\n  signs of a more active pattern returning late in the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Halloween.\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a trough overhead with a vort max over \nsouthern CA in the process of pinching off into a closed low. Behind \nthe shortwave, gusty north winds are currently pushing through the \nsouthern Great Basin and into the northern Mojave Desert. These will \ncontinue advance southward, encompassing most of the CWA by sunrise. \nStrongest winds anticipated in Death Valley and the southern Owens \nValley where gusts of 40-50 mph are possible through the morning \nhours. Elsewhere, expecting gusts generally 20-35 mph, weakening \nduring the 2nd half of the day. The formation of the closed low will \ncause the system to slowly exit the region, lingering over \nnorthwestern AZ this afternoon. The cold air aloft, residual \nmoisture, and daytime heating should drive isolated to scattered \nshowers and maybe even a few storms over eastern Mohave County (20-\n40%), with lower chances (10-20%) over southeastern NV and the \nColorado River Valley. Can't completely rule out an isolated \ninstance of flash flooding in eastern Mohave County, but odds are 5% \nat best.\n\nOur low finally exits on Sunday, leaving us in a zonal pattern for a \nfew days before transitioning to southwesterly flow later in the \nweek. From Sunday - Thursday, the forecast calls for seasonable \ntemperatures, light breezes, and dry conditions. Late in the week, \nensemble guidance shows an energized subtropical jet trying to push \nmoisture into the southwestern US. Cluster analysis shows several \nvariations of offshore troughing during this time, so there remains \na fair amount of uncertainty, but there appears to be a good signal \nfor the existence of an offshore trough and southwesterly flow over \nus. Should this come to fruition, we should see an increase in \nsouthwest winds and low to moderate (10-50%) precipitation chances \naround Halloween weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Gusty \nnortherly winds are arriving with gusts peaking around 25 kts \nthrough the mid-afternoon. Winds will remain northerly, but gusts \ncut off in the mid-afternoon. Into the night, expect winds to reduce \nslightly and turn westerly before becoming light and variable into \nthe late night. Skies remain mostly clear through the TAF period.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...The line of gustier \nwinds has already exited the western portions of the region, meaning \nKBIH and KDAG will keep lighter winds through the TAF period. Breezy \nwinds are building for KVGT and KHND with strong winds arriving to \nKEED and KIFP in the mid morning. Winds will begin to reduce into \nthe afternoon with gusts cutting off into the evening and early \nnight. Expect winds across the region to become light into the late \nnight. Skies remain mostly clear through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
